  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1228 AM MDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nWe are expecting more waves of precipitation, especially outside\nof the central mountains...with the heaviest through tomorrow \nmorning. The \"drier\" spots still appear to be across the Lava\nBeds north through the Wood River Valley and Stanley Basin, as\nwell as north toward Challis. Additional amounts of 0.30-0.60\"\nare expected through Monday afternoon. Otherwise, we are still\nlooking at widespread 0.75-1.5\" of precipitation, with some\nspots pushing 2\" along and east of I-15. The 90th percentile\n(high end) of the range in the Blend of Models has the potential\nfor 1.25-2.25\", and the absolute highest is still going with\nanother 1.5-3.0\", pushing 3.5\"+ along and east of I-15 in a few\nspots. We are also still looking at some snow across the central\nmountains and along the Montana border. Other than some light\nsnow in the Stanley and Copper Basins, snow accumulation will \nbe limited to above 7500-8000ft. We might even see up to 6\" \nacross highest peaks and ranges (above 9500-10000ft). The Blend \nis trying to sneak some light snow at highest elevations of the \nSouth Hills and Albion Mountains. That seems to be a function of\na pocket of colder air and heavier precipitation in that \nparticular area. IF we do see accumulations there, it will be \nshort-lived tonight and early Monday morning. Showers will \ncontinue Tuesday into Wednesday, with precipitation gradually \nending northwest to southeast...as the low slows to almost a \ncrawl as it swings across Utah into Colorado and Wyoming. \n\nA blocking pattern with high pressure over Idaho is expected to\nbuild, as a large area of low pressure develops east of the \nRockies. Most of the models (ensembles and AI version as well)\ndevelop a cutoff low across the Desert Southwest/Baja next week\nas part of the blocking pattern. The ECMWF wants to actually to\nstop the low over Colorado, and show it retrograding west back\nacross Utah and Nevada. That seems to be an odd outlier for\nsure. Highs will gradually warm back into the 60s for valley\nlocations under the ridge.",
